<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Town Square</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Look at the Traditional and Modern Building Materials.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify specific information in descriptions and narratives. Interactive: Respond flexibly to changes in group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can distinguish between key facts and minor details in spoken narratives about the town (e.g., "The town square has a fountain."). Can follow spoken directions to locate places on a map (e.g., "Where is the library?"). Can adapt to changes in group discussions about map directions by clarifying and confirming details with peers (e.g., "I thought the library was next to the school. Can you show me again on the map?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a town square with both traditional (e.g., old stone monument) and modern (e.g., glass building) elements. Students individually observe the picture. The teacher asks, "What do you see in the town square?" and elicits simple answers like "a fountain," "tall buildings," "a park." Then, the teacher asks, "Are the buildings old or new?" to introduce the theme of traditional and modern materials.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to different elements in the picture (e.g., a 'street', a 'statue', a 'roof'). While pointing, the teacher clearly says the word and models its pronunciation, emphasizing consonant clusters for words like "street" /striːt/ and syllable stress for "monument" /ˈmɒn.jə.mənt/. The teacher says, "There is a fountain in the middle of the town square. There are many buildings around it." The teacher demonstrates listening by asking a student to point to a specific item after hearing the word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'fountain' for sitting or for water?" (Expected: For water.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'street,' what sound do you hear at the beginning?" (Expected: The /s/ sound.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Town Square")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a short narrative (approx. 1 minute) describing a guided tour around "El Parque Central" (The Central Park/Plaza) in a Panamanian town. The narrative uses `there is/are`, `WH-questions` for asking about places, `prepositions of place`, and `imperatives` for simple directions. It highlights traditional buildings (e.g., the old church, town hall with specific roofs) and modern structures (e.g., a new library, busy market). Key vocabulary from the list is embedded.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifies specific information and main ideas in short, simple spoken narratives and descriptions related to daily life and the immediate environment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listen):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen for the main idea. "What is the speaker describing?" (Expected: A town square/plaza.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listen):</b> The teacher provides a simple map of a town square with unlabeled buildings. Before playing the audio a second time, the teacher gives students 3-4 True/False statements based on the audio, e.g., "There is a modern statue in front of the old church." "The market is next to the library." Students listen and mark T or F.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Third Listen):</b> The teacher provides a list of key vocabulary words (e.g., `fountain`, `traditional building`, `modern library`, `market`) and asks students to listen again and point to where they think these places might be on the unlabeled map as they hear them mentioned.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for specific details using `there is/there are`, `WH-questions` for asking about places, `prepositions of place` (e.g., next to, in front of, across from, on the corner of), and `imperatives` (e.g., walk, turn, cross, follow, pass) within the context of the town square scenario and traditional/modern buildings. Vocabulary: `town square, map, statue, fountain, street, building, roof, tradition, park, library, market, school, crosswalk, traffic light, sidewalk, town hall, pedestrian bridge, historic, modern, busy, quiet`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Listen and Complete:</b> The teacher plays parts of the audio from Stage 2 again, pausing at key sentences. Students have a worksheet with sentences from the narrative, but with gaps for target vocabulary or prepositions. E.g., "The old church is _______ the park." "There is a _______ building next to the market." Students listen and write the missing words. (Focus on `there is/are`, prepositions, target nouns/adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Map Labeling Dictation:</b> In pairs, students receive a blank map of a small town square (similar to the one from Stage 2 but with more detail) with numbered outlines of buildings. The teacher dictates short sentences or descriptions, one at a time, using imperatives and prepositions. E.g., "Number 1: It is a tall, modern library. It is across from the fountain." "Number 2: This is the historic town hall. It is next to the park." Students listen and write the correct label next to the corresponding number on their map. (Focus on `there is/are`, prepositions, adjectives, nouns, and pronunciation of /s/ clusters).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: Listen and Confirm:</b> Students work with their partner to compare their labeled maps. One student reads their label for a numbered building, and the partner listens and confirms, "Yes, that's correct. The library is across from the fountain." or "No, I think the market is there. Can you say it again?" This activity encourages active listening and clarification. (Focus on listening for accuracy, `WH-questions` like "Where is...?", and `responding flexibly`).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Map-Following Challenge. Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives an identical, more detailed map of "The Town Square" with several landmarks (e.g., library, park, market, traditional church, modern art gallery, pedestrian bridge, traffic light) but with some paths marked by dotted lines and specific destinations unmarked.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays an audio recording of complex directions from a starting point (e.g., "You are at the park entrance") to an unknown destination (e.g., "the old fountain" or "the new museum").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students in the group listen collaboratively. They must trace the path on their map using their finger or a pencil as they listen. The audio includes imperatives and sequencing words (e.g., "First, walk straight ahead. Then, turn left at the traffic light. Next, cross the street. You will pass a traditional building. The destination is in front of the modern art gallery.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After listening, groups discuss, "Where did we go?" "I think we went to the library. No, the library is next to the school. Can you play that part again?" They must collectively agree on the final destination and mark it on their map.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a second set of directions leading to another location, reinforcing collaborative listening and clarification.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening for complex directions, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion (correctly identifying the final destination on the map based on spoken instructions). Students adapt to changes by clarifying and confirming details with peers.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Quick Map Quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays one final short audio clip (20-30 seconds) describing the location of 2-3 specific new buildings (e.g., "There is a small cafe across from the old cinema. It has a bright red roof."). Students listen and quickly mark the locations on a simplified version of the town square map provided individually. The teacher collects these maps for a quick check.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">During the group map-following challenge, the teacher uses an observation checklist to note:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active listening (e.g., engaged, tracing on map).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responding flexibly to changes in discussions (e.g., asking for clarification, confirming details with peers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to distinguish key facts (locations, directions).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in locating places on the map (from the final audio quiz).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated listening comprehension by following multi-step directions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation in group discussions (observed).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of key vocabulary (e.g., prepositions, building names).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick slip of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word you heard today about the town?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to follow the directions today? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to directions is most challenging for you (e.g., understanding prepositions, remembering the order of steps)?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you improve your listening skills next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What listening activities worked best for student engagement and comprehension? (e.g., the map-labeling dictation, group map challenge).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the audio inputs at the appropriate CEFR A2.2 level, specifically in terms of speed and complexity of directions/descriptions? What adjustments are needed for clarity (e.g., more pauses, clearer pronunciation)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully use the target vocabulary and grammar (prepositions, imperatives) while listening and clarifying?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What scaffolds (e.g., visual aids, repeating instructions, peer support) were most effective?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This lesson introduced key vocabulary and listening for specific information about the town square. For Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a written text that uses the same vocabulary and describes the town square in more detail, preparing them to read for similar information.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[3-5 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>